5.MTBF:
MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures) is the average time between system failures. A higher MTBF indicates a more stable and resilient system,
meaning failures occur less frequently.
We improve MTBF by strengthening system design, implementing redundancy, and performing proactive monitoring and preventive maintenance.
__________________________________________________________________________________________-
6.Error budjet:
Error Budget is the amount of allowable failure or downtime defined by a service’s SLO over a specific time period. 
It represents how much unreliability the system can tolerate while still meeting its reliability target.
If we exhaust the error budget, we pause new feature releases and focus on improving system stability and reliability.
Error budgets help teams balance innovation and reliability by enabling controlled risk while protecting customer experience.
___________________________________________________________________________________________
7.RPO;
RPO (Recovery Point Objective) defines the maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time after a failure.
It helps determine backup frequency and data protection strategies to minimize business impact.
____________________________________________________________________________
8.RTO:
RTO (Recovery Time Objective) defines the maximum acceptable downtime after a failure. 
It helps guide disaster recovery planning, automation, and restoration procedures to ensure rapid service recovery.
_____________________________________________________________________
D/f of rto & rpo:

RPO defines the maximum acceptable data loss after a failure, while RTO defines the maximum acceptable time to restore the system.
RPO focuses on data protection, while RTO focuses on service restoration time. Together, they guide disaster recovery planning and 
business continuity strategies.
